{"type":"message","id":"794c455a","parentId":"d2307180","timestamp":"2026-02-01T17:17:10.875Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Create a comprehensive learning guide + project build at /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/clawdbot-project-guide/\n\nThis is a \"Learn Clawdbot by Building Something Real\" tutorial. The reader is someone who just set up Clawdbot on EC2 (using our setup guide) and wants to learn how to actually USE it to build projects. The example project is a 2D Image → 3D Printable STL web tool.\n\n## Files to create:\n\n### 1. README.md\nBrief overview: what this is, who it's for, what you'll learn, what you'll build.\n\n### 2. GUIDE.md — \"Learn Clawdbot by Building Something Real\"\nThe main tutorial. Written in second person (\"you\"), conversational, encouraging but not patronizing. Assumes technical reader who's new to working WITH an AI agent.\n\n#### Chapter 1: The Interview (Requirements Gathering)\nTeach the reader HOW to interview their AI to scope a project:\n- Why you don't just say \"build me an app\" (garbage in, garbage out)\n- The interview framework:\n  1. **Use Case** — What problem are we solving? Who's it for?\n  2. **Scope** — What's in v1, what's not? Where's the boundary?\n  3. **Input/Output** — What goes in, what comes out? Be specific.\n  4. **Technical Constraints** — Browser-only? Needs a server? Specific APIs?\n  5. **Quality Bar** — What does \"done\" look like?\n  6. **Edge Cases** — What could go wrong? What weird inputs might users try?\n- Show this AS A CONVERSATION. Write the actual back-and-forth for the 3D tool:\n  - Human: \"I want to convert 2D images to 3D printable STL files\"\n  - AI asks clarifying questions (what types of images? what complexity? web tool or CLI?)\n  - Human answers, AI refines understanding\n  - Keep going until requirements are crisp\n- End with a filled-out PROJECT BRIEF that summarizes everything decided\n\n#### Chapter 2: Architecture & Model Selection\nTeach how to think about architecture WITH your AI:\n- Ask your AI to propose 2-3 architecture options with tradeoffs\n- For the 3D tool:\n  - Option A: 100% client-side (Three.js + STL export in browser)\n  - Option B: Client + server (upload image, server processes, returns STL)\n  - Option C: Client + AI depth estimation API\n- How to pick: start simple, add complexity only when needed\n- **Model selection guide:**\n  - Opus/Sonnet 4: Architecture decisions, complex problem solving, code review\n  - Sonnet: Day-to-day building, most coding tasks, debugging\n  - Haiku: Simple tasks, formatting, boilerplate, routine checks\n  - Show when to use /model command or spawn with specific model\n- Decision for this project: Option A (client-side) for v1, Option C as future enhancement\n\n#### Chapter 3: The Build Plan\nTeach how to break a project into sub-agent tasks:\n- Why you don't just say \"build the whole thing\" in one prompt\n- How to decompose: identify independent pieces that can be built in parallel\n- For the 3D tool, the breakdown:\n  1. **Task 1:** Core HTML/CSS — upload UI, 3D preview container, controls panel, download button\n  2. **Task 2:** Image processing — load image, convert to grayscale heightmap, apply filters\n  3. **Task 3:** 3D mesh generation — Three.js scene, generate geometry from heightmap, lighting, camera controls\n  4. **Task 4:** STL export — generate binary STL from mesh, trigger download\n  5. **Task 5:** Integration & polish — connect all pieces, responsive design, error handling\n- Teach: which tasks can be parallel (1+2), which are sequential (3 needs 2, 4 needs 3)\n- Show the actual sub-agent spawn commands with good task prompts\n\n#### Chapter 4: Writing Great Task Prompts\nThis is the KEY skill. Teach how to write prompts that produce good code:\n- **Be specific about output** — file paths, function signatures, module format\n- **Provide context** — what other files exist, what interfaces to match\n- **Set constraints** — \"no external dependencies\", \"must work in browser\", \"use ES modules\"\n- **Include test criteria** — \"it should be able to handle images up to 4K resolution\"\n- **Show bad vs good prompt examples:**\n  - BAD: \"Build the image processing part\"\n  - GOOD: [Write a detailed, specific prompt for Task 2 that would actually produce great code]\n- Show 2-3 real task prompts for the 3D tool build\n\n#### Chapter 5: Review & Iterate\nTeach how to review AI-generated code:\n- Don't just trust it — test it\n- What to look for: edge cases, error handling, performance, security\n- How to give feedback: be specific about what's wrong, not just \"fix it\"\n- When to iterate vs rewrite\n- The review loop: spawn → review → feedback → fix → test → approve\n\n#### Chapter 6: Deploy & Share\nTeach how to get the project live:\n- PM2 for process management\n- Cloudflare tunnels for public access\n- Basic monitoring (pm2 logs, health checks)\n- How to share with others\n\n#### Chapter 7: What's Next\n- How to plan v2 features\n- When to add complexity (AI depth estimation, server-side processing)\n- How to turn a project into a product (landing page, users, monetization)\n- The compound effect: each project teaches you to work better with your AI\n\n### 3. PROJECT-BRIEF.md — The filled-out project brief for the 3D tool\nThis is the output of Chapter 1's interview process. A clean, structured document:\n- Project name: \"MeshForge\" (or pick something cool)\n- Problem statement\n- Target users\n- Core features (v1)\n- Future features (v2+)\n- Technical architecture\n- Input/output specs\n- Quality bar / acceptance criteria\n- Edge cases and error handling\n\n### 4. TASK-PROMPTS.md — Ready-to-use sub-agent prompts\nThe actual prompts from Chapter 4, formatted so the reader can copy-paste them:\n- Task 1 prompt (UI shell)\n- Task 2 prompt (image processing)  \n- Task 3 prompt (3D mesh generation)\n- Task 4 prompt (STL export)\n- Task 5 prompt (integration)\nEach prompt should be detailed enough that spawning it as a sub-agent would produce working code.\n\n### 5. Add to dashboard\nUpdate /home/ec2-user/clawd/dashboard/api/links-config.json to add:\n- \"clawdbot-project-guide\" document pointing to GUIDE.md\n- \"meshforge-brief\" document pointing to PROJECT-BRIEF.md\n\n## Writing Guidelines:\n- Conversational, encouraging, not corporate\n- Use real code examples and real commands\n- Include \"💡 Pro tip\" and \"⚠️ Common mistake\" callouts\n- The reader should feel like they're learning from a friend who's done this before\n- Don't dumb it down — assume smart people who are new to AI-assisted development\n- Make it feel exciting: \"You're about to build something in hours that would take days alone\"\n- Reference the setup guide for prerequisites\n- Total guide should be thorough but not bloated — ~5000-8000 words for GUIDE.md\n\n## IMPORTANT:\n- Do NOT actually build the 3D tool code — just the guide and prompts\n- The whole point is that THEIR Clawdbot builds it using the prompts\n- Make the task prompts genuinely good — they should produce working code when spawned\n- No real API keys or personal info"}],"timestamp":1769966230873}}
